What the RGPL actually does, mechanically
-----------------------------------------

The RGPL descends from the GPL family, and it keeps what that family does:
anybody may run, read, modify, and redistribute this. If they pass it on, they
pass it on under this same license, with source. They cannot add restrictions
on top. There is no warranty. Their patent rights in what they contribute pass
along with the code.

It also keeps the Affero line's answer to a gap in the ordinary GPL. Under the
ordinary GPL, the obligation to hand over source is triggered only by giving
somebody a copy. A program run on your own machine and reached over a network
never changes hands, so nobody using it is ever owed its source.

The RGPL closes that gap more widely, with one rule of its own, which is the
entire reason to pick it over any other: our clause, section 13.

Using the program *with* another person is not private use. If someone else is
using it, operating it, or interacting with it -- or reaching you, or anyone
else, through it -- in the same room or over a network, modified or not, you
owe that person the source of the version you are running, at no charge. It
must go straight from you to them: through the program itself, over the same
connection, or by some other direct hand-off. It may not be routed through, held
by, or made conditional on any third party, and no third party may be told it
was asked for or given.

Two things do not trigger it. Using the program alone, by yourself, owes
nothing. And the things the program makes -- a file, an image, a printed page --
travel free once they are made; handing someone a result is not using the
program with them.

There is a second rule of its own, section 14: reports. Whoever passes the
program on, or uses it with someone else, must keep a way open for anyone to
report what is wrong with it -- no account, no fee, no password -- and must
display every report they receive, either in public or in a file named REPORTS
that travels with the source. They never have to fix anything or answer
anyone. What they cannot do is hide that a fault was reported. Security flaws
may be held back for up to ninety days so they can be fixed first; personal
information may be cut, with the cut marked; a reporter can ask to stay
nameless; and bulk mail can be set aside, but the number set aside is shown.

The practical effect is that this work stays open in every direction it can
travel, and in every room it is used in. Someone can build on it, including
commercially; what they cannot do is close it, or put it between themselves and
another person while keeping its workings to themselves.

Work in this repository that I did not write
--------------------------------------------

I cannot license code I did not write, and I am not going to pretend otherwise.

Anything in these directories that came from somewhere else is covered by
whatever license its original author gave it, not by mine. Where a directory
carries its own license file, that file governs that directory and this one
does not reach inside it. This applies to vendored dependencies, upstream
checkouts, downloaded art assets, toolchains, and anything else that arrived
here already licensed -- including cases where the original terms are
proprietary and could not be combined with the RGPL even if I wanted to.

It also applies to a few of my own projects that carry a different license of
their own choosing. Those are mine either way, so there is no conflict; the
subdirectory license simply wins where one exists.

The rule is: look for the nearest license file, walking up from whatever you
are holding, and read the file's own header while you are at it -- plenty of
single-file libraries carry their entire grant inline as a comment and never
ship a separate license file at all. If you find terms before you reach this
directory, those are the ones that apply. If you get all the way here without
finding anything, the work is mine and it is RGPL-1.0-or-later.

One correction to that rule, and it matters more than the rule does: a missing
license file does not make somebody else's work mine. If a vendored library or
a downloaded asset landed here without its license attached, that is a bug in
how I stored it, not a transfer of ownership. It stays under whatever terms its
author actually granted, and the fix is to go put the license file where it
belongs. Nothing in this document is a claim over work I did not do.

A standing offer to any artist whose work is here
-------------------------------------------------

If you made art that one of these projects uses -- a painting, a drawing, music,
a sound, a font, a typeface, a map, writing, anything I would have had to ask a
person for -- then everything that project is, is yours for the asking.

Ask, and I will send you the whole project: the source code, the documents, the
issue files, the design notes, the tooling, the LLM transcripts, and the git
history of that project's directory. Not a release build and not a tidied-up
snapshot -- the working material, in whatever state it is actually in. And with
it, permission to do anything you want with what you have been sent, under no
conditions at all: build on it, sell it, close it, take it apart, ship it under
your own name, license it however you like, or throw it away. You do not owe me
the source of your version, you do not owe me credit, and you do not owe me a
reason for asking. The one thing with a rule attached is handing this on to
somebody else, and that rule has a bullet of its own further down.

The scope is the projects that use your art, each one entire. Not this monorepo.
If your painting is in one game, that game is the thing you can have; the dozens
of other directories in here are not part of the offer, because they have
nothing to do with you.

Now the parts of this that are not mine to give, stated plainly, because an
offer with an unmarked hole in it is worse than a smaller offer honestly drawn.

- **Other people's work travels under its own terms.** Vendored libraries,
  toolchains, downloaded assets: I can hand you the copy I have, and it arrives
  under whatever license its author actually granted, exactly as it does for
  anybody else. My grant covers my work and only my work.

- **Another artist's art is theirs, and not mine to sign over to you.** If two
  people made art for the same project, each of you can have everything I did,
  and neither of you receives the other's work through me. Ask them yourself;
  they may well say yes.

- **Ownership here is whole, and it is shared.** You do not get a slice of this
  sized to how much of it you made. You made part of it, so you own all of it --
  the same all of it that I own. The one thing you cannot get is exclusive
  title, and that is a decision rather than a limit I ran into: this should never
  be owned by one person or a few. The code is already published under the RGPL
  and cannot be un-published, and if a second artist asks I will say yes to them
  on the same terms without hesitating. Nobody's ownership shrinks when another
  person's begins. What this is made of is not in finite supply, and there is no
  version of this where somebody else having all of it leaves you with less.

  Concretely, what that ownership is made of: a permanent, irrevocable, no-cost
  grant, sitting on top of the RGPL and free of every condition the RGPL would
  otherwise impose, of everything I as the copyright holder am able to do with
  the work. The practical difference is the whole of it -- the RGPL says you
  must pass the source along, and this says you do not have to.

- **It is transferrable once, and after that only by asking.** It is not stapled
  to your person. You may sell it, or give it, to one other person. One. That
  power is single-use: spending it spends it, and there is no second one behind
  it. What it costs you is the power to do it again -- not the thing itself. You
  still own everything you owned the day before, because you own it for having
  made the art, and no sale changes the fact that you made the art. And what you
  *make* with it is yours in the ordinary way, to sell, license, or transfer as
  often and as freely as you please. That is your work; this rule is not about
  your work.

  It has to land somewhere it will be used. You cannot sell it to somebody who
  cannot use it -- not to a broker, not to a shell, not to anyone acquiring it in
  order to sit on it. This is a seat at a workbench and not an instrument, and it
  is void the moment it is parked.

  Whoever you hand it to arrives owning everything you own, and arrives with no
  onward sale of their own. They cannot sell it. They cannot trade it. What they
  can do is ask me first, before anything moves -- and the most I am able to say
  yes to is their giving it away. Never their selling it on. A sale stops dead at
  the person who bought it. This holds at every further remove: three steps down
  the chain, money or no money, gift or trade, the rule is ask the royalty first.

Two things that shape is doing, since neither falls out of the rules by reading
them.

It keeps the freedom moving on foot rather than by post. Every move is one
person handing it to one person who will use it, which is slow, and slow is the
whole point -- a freedom that can be forwarded arrives everywhere at once, and
then it is not an offer to artists any more, it is a permissive license with
extra steps and the copyleft has quietly evaporated for everybody. The copyleft
is here to stop this being fenced off by somebody who did none of the work. It
was never meant to hold a door shut against the person who painted it, and it is
not meant to swing open for everyone standing behind them either.

And it keeps me reachable. Every move past the first comes back through me,
which is not a toll -- there is nothing to pay, and no royalty for the royalty
to collect except the asking. It is that somebody ought to be able to find out
where a copy went, and the only way that stays true is if there is a person
standing at every link in the chain.

There is no form, no fee, no NDA, and no negotiation. Email gabrilend@gmail.com
and say which project. It applies backwards to art that is already here, it does
not expire, I cannot revoke it, and asking does not end anything -- you may ask
and keep working, or ask and walk away, or ask years later, and it costs you
nothing in any of those cases.

Why this exists: I am doing software design, not building a product. A project
in here is worth more to me as a thing that got designed than as a thing whose
copies I control. Art is the one part I cannot do myself and cannot generate my
way around, and somebody who makes it should not end up with a weaker claim on
the result than I have.

Where the source comes from, and why it comes from a person
------------------------------------------------------------

Section 13 of the RGPL says that whoever you use this program with owes you its
source, and must give it to you directly. So the answer to "where do I get the
source?" is always the same: from the person you got the program from, or from
the person you are using it with, or from the program itself.

There is deliberately no central address to fetch it from. A copy served from a
host -- whether it is the running system's own "Source" link or a public mirror
somewhere -- puts a third party between two people. That third party can watch
who asks, can go away, can be told what to serve, and can quietly stand in for
the truth. A host is a convenience, never the authority. If you want to check
that the source you were handed is the source you are running, compare the two
directly: build what you were given and see whether it behaves the same, or ask
the person who handed it to you to show you.

The kindest program carries its own source and knows how to give it -- a
command, a menu item, or a step in the first handshake between two copies,
sending it straight from one machine to the other. Where a project in here can
do that, that is the preferred route. Where it cannot yet, the source is handed
over by hand: a copy of the directory, on whatever medium the two of you use.

To get the source from me, ask me directly. rmail, the mail system I am
building, is the channel I would reach for: it goes from one machine to one
machine with nobody in the middle. Plain email to gabrilend@gmail.com also
works. Either way I send the source straight to you.

The same holds for everyone else. Someone who takes this, changes it, and uses
it with other people owes those people the source of *their* version, handed
over the same way: from them, directly, with nobody in between.

